{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Aggregation Pattern.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Aggregation Pattern.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"aggregation pattern aggregation pattern","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Aggregation Pattern.md","fileName":"Aggregation Pattern.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Aggregation Pattern","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-27 00:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[.NET Server Concurrency and Parallel Patterns]]","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Server Concurrency and Parallel Patterns","hands-onparallelprogrammingwithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Aggregation Pattern.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Aggregation Pattern.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"aggregation pattern aggregation pattern","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Aggregation Pattern.md","fileName":"Aggregation Pattern.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Aggregation Pattern","heading":"Aggregation Pattern","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The aggregation pattern combines partial results produced by independent parallel operations into one result. Sum, count, minimum, and grouped summaries are common examples when the combining operation can be applied incrementally.\n\nEfficient aggregation keeps intermediate state local to workers and performs a controlled reduction instead of locking one shared accumulator for every input. Associativity makes regrouping safe; order-sensitive or nonassociative operations require a more explicit merge plan.","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Server Concurrency and Parallel Patterns","hands-onparallelprogrammingwithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Aggregation Pattern.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Aggregation Pattern.m